\section{Method}
\label{sec:method}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/pipeline.pdf}
\caption{Generational depth estimation pipeline. Seed documents are recursively passed through a generation operator $G_\delta$ to produce a depth chain. Statistical features are fed to the OBD-LightGBM classifier for depth prediction.}
\label{fig:pipeline}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Problem Formulation}
\label{sec:formulation}

A generation operator $G_\delta$ (LLM + decoding strategy $\delta$) produces successive generations $X \to Y_1 \to \cdots \to Y_K$, where $Y_k = G_\delta(Y_{k-1})$ and $Y_0 \triangleq X$ (Figure~\ref{fig:pipeline}).
This sequence forms a Markov chain.
By the Data Processing Inequality, $I(X; Y_1) \geq I(X; Y_2) \geq \cdots \geq 0$: the original signal degrades monotonically.
Together with Markov chain convergence, this guarantees a finite $\Ktheory$ beyond which adjacent generations are indistinguishable.

We define the operational lower bound:
\begin{equation}
  \Kempirical = \max\{k : \text{CI}_{\text{lower}}^{95\%}(\auc_{k,k+1}) > \theta\},
  \label{eq:kstar}
\end{equation}
where $\theta = 0.60$ is justified by the permutation null distribution (95th percentile $< 0.55$ across all configurations; bootstrap: 1{,}000 resamples).

\subsection{Features and Classifier}
\label{sec:features}

Each document is characterized by a 15-dimensional feature vector (Table~\ref{tab:features}; full definitions in Table~\ref{tab:s5_features}): surprisal moments and derivatives (9~dims), vocabulary diversity (3~dims), and frequency tail statistics (3~dims).
All features are computed from token-level surprisal values via an external scorer model.
Hand-crafted features enable cross-generator transfer without retraining and permit attribution of discriminative power to specific text properties.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{The 15-dimensional feature set.}
\label{tab:features}
\begin{tabular}{lll}
\toprule
\textbf{Group} & \textbf{Feature} & \textbf{Description} \\
\midrule
\multirow{4}{*}{\parbox{1.8cm}{Surprisal\\moments}}
  & \texttt{surp\_mean}      & Mean token surprisal \\
  & \texttt{surp\_std}       & Std.\ dev. \\
  & \texttt{surp\_skew}      & Skewness \\
  & \texttt{surp\_kurt}      & Kurtosis \\
\midrule
\multirow{5}{*}{\parbox{1.8cm}{Surprisal\\derivatives}}
  & \texttt{surp\_d1\_mean}  & Mean 1st-order diff. \\
  & \texttt{surp\_d1\_std}   & Std.\ of 1st-order diff. \\
  & \texttt{surp\_d1\_skew}  & Skewness of 1st-order diff. \\
  & \texttt{surp\_d2\_mean}  & Mean 2nd-order diff. \\
  & \texttt{surp\_d2\_std}   & Std.\ of 2nd-order diff. \\
\midrule
\multirow{3}{*}{\parbox{1.8cm}{Vocab.\\diversity}}
  & \texttt{ttr}             & Type-token ratio \\
  & \texttt{hapax\_ratio}    & Hapax legomena ratio \\
  & \texttt{self\_bleu}      & 4-gram self-BLEU \\
\midrule
\multirow{3}{*}{\parbox{1.8cm}{Freq.\\tail}}
  & \texttt{freq\_kurtosis}  & Frequency kurtosis \\
  & \texttt{freq\_entropy}   & Frequency entropy \\
  & \texttt{low\_freq\_ratio} & Rare token fraction \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

We use \emph{Ordinal Binary Decomposition} (OBD), decomposing the $(K{+}1)$-class problem into $K$ cumulative binary sub-classifiers, each estimating $P(\text{depth} > k \mid \mathbf{x})$ via LightGBM (hyperparameters in Table~\ref{tab:s6_lgbm_params}).
OBD achieves lower MAE than multiclass LightGBM (e.g., 0.984 vs.\ 1.016 on Qwen-rewrite) while maintaining superior pairwise $\auc$ (mean 0.6095 vs.\ 0.4883 for ordinal logistic regression).

\subsection{Experimental Setup}
\label{sec:experimental_setup}

We evaluate four configurations spanning three model families (Table~\ref{tab:setup}): Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct (rewrite and continuation modes), Pythia-1.4B and OLMo-1B (continuation).
Each uses 5{,}000 C4 seeds truncated to 256 tokens, recursively generated for $K{=}5$ iterations (30{,}000 documents per configuration).
Default decoding: nucleus $p{=}0.95$, $T{=}1.0$; decoding ablation covers greedy, $T{=}0.7$, and $T{=}1.2$.
Continuation mode truncates seeds to 128 tokens and extends to 256; rewrite mode paraphrases the full text.
Evaluation: 5-fold GroupKFold cross-validation grouped by seed document; pairwise $\auc$ as primary metric.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Experimental configurations.}
\label{tab:setup}
\begin{tabular}{llcc}
\toprule
\textbf{Config} & \textbf{Model} & \textbf{Mode} & $N$ \\
\midrule
Qwen-rw   & Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct & Rewrite & 5{,}000 \\
Qwen-cont & Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct & Continuation & 5{,}000 \\
Pythia    & Pythia-1.4B            & Continuation & 5{,}000 \\
OLMo      & OLMo-1B               & Continuation & 5{,}000 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\section{Related Work}
\label{sec:related_work}

\paragraph{Model Collapse.}
Shumailov et al.~\cite{shumailov2024collapse} showed that iterative training on model outputs causes irreversible tail-distribution degradation.
Alemohammad et al.~\cite{alemohammad2024selfconsuming} proved convergence to a fixed point, and Dohmatob et al.~\cite{dohmatob2024model} derived scaling laws for tail probability loss.
Schaeffer et al.~\cite{schaeffer2025position} organized the literature into eight definitions; our $\Kstar$ aligns with their Family~B (D4: collapsing variance, D6: mode entanglement).
Kazdan et al.~\cite{kazdan2025collapse} distinguished replace and accumulate regimes; our prompt-chain operates in the replace regime, making $\Kempirical = 2$ a conservative lower bound.
These works characterize macro dynamics but provide no per-text depth estimation tools.

\paragraph{Binary Synthetic Text Detection.}
Detection methods---DetectGPT~\cite{mitchell2023detectgpt}, Fast-DetectGPT~\citep{bao2024fast}, Binoculars~\cite{hans2024binoculars}, DivEye~\cite{basani2026diveye}, Min-K\%++~\cite{zhang2024minkpp}, CoDeC~\cite{zawalski2026codec}---achieve strong human-vs.-machine separation but produce no depth estimate.
DivEye's surprisal diversity features overlap with our \texttt{surp\_*} subset, but optimize for binary detection rather than ordinal depth discrimination.
Recent work moves beyond binary labels along other axes: collaboration degree~\cite{cheng2025beyond} and contamination type hierarchies~\cite{mehta2025beyond}, but neither addresses recursive generation depth.

\paragraph{Data Selection.}
DSIR~\cite{xie2023dsir}, D4~\cite{tirumala2023d4}, and DoReMi~\cite{xie2023doremi} improve corpus quality through distribution matching but ignore recursive depth.
Drayson et al.~\cite{drayson2025} combined binary detection with stratified importance resampling---a special case of our framework when $\Kstar = 1$.
Feng et al.~\cite{feng2025beyond} showed external verification can prevent collapse; our $\Kstar$ can inform such verifiers by delineating the depth frontier below which quality discrimination remains feasible.

\paragraph{Ordinal Classification and Probing.}
Ordinal regression~\cite{frank2001ordinal, cao2020coral} exploits label ordering but has not been applied to synthetic text depth.
Our OBD head follows Frank \& Hall's binary decomposition principle.
Probing classifiers~\cite{alain2017understanding, conneau2018probing, belinkov2022probing} analyze pretrained representations; our neural probe follows this protocol with a controlled-capacity MLP on frozen embeddings.

\section{Application: $\Kstar$-Aware Data Filtering}
\label{sec:filtering}

$\Kempirical = 2$ correctly predicts that graduated refinement beyond the binary boundary provides no additional benefit over binary filtering.
We compare six data curation strategies on a corpus of 30{,}000 documents (5{,}000 per depth, depths 0--5): (i)~\textbf{no-filter} (uniform weight), (ii)~\textbf{binary-ours} ($w = 1 - P(\text{synthetic})$ from the 15D LightGBM classifier), (iii)~\textbf{binary-Drayson} (importance weighting following \citet{drayson2025}, same classifier, weights $w_j \propto (1 - q(x_j))^b$, $b{=}1$, cap $r_{\max}{=}3$), (iv)~\textbf{graduated} (exponential decay $w = \exp(-\alpha \cdot \hat{d})$, $\alpha \in \{0.1,\ldots,0.9\}$), (v)~\textbf{surprisal} (retain documents above corpus median surprisal), and (vi)~\textbf{DSIR}~\citep{xie2023dsir} (importance resampling against depth-0 reference).

We fine-tune Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct via LoRA (rank~8, $\alpha_{\text{LoRA}}{=}16$) for 3 epochs, evaluating on MMLU (14{,}042 examples, 0-shot) and HellaSwag (10{,}042 examples, 0-shot) across 5 seeds; the graduated strategy is further crossed with 5~$\alpha$ values (51 total runs).

\paragraph{Results.}

\input{tables/tab_filtering_main}

All strategies degrade absolute performance relative to the base model (MMLU 0.6018, HellaSwag 0.6821).
Binary-ours achieves the highest accuracy on both benchmarks (MMLU $0.5941 \pm 0.0009$; HellaSwag $0.6701 \pm 0.0006$), outperforming no-filter by $+$0.76\,pp / $+$0.63\,pp and binary-Drayson ($0.5913$; $0.6691$) by $+$0.28\,pp / $+$0.10\,pp.
Graduated filtering at its best $\alpha$ does not outperform binary-ours on either benchmark.
Surprisal-based selection (MMLU $0.5894 \pm 0.0010$; HellaSwag $0.6641 \pm 0.0006$) falls short by $-$0.47\,pp / $-$0.60\,pp.
DSIR results (\textbf{XX.XX}) are pending.

\paragraph{$\alpha$ sensitivity.}
Higher $\alpha$ values yield monotonically better performance on both benchmarks (HellaSwag: $0.6640$ at $\alpha{=}0.1$ to $0.6701$ at $\alpha{=}0.9$).
At $\alpha{=}0.9$, graduated filtering narrows the MMLU gap to 0.49\,pp below binary-ours ($0.5892$ vs.\ $0.5941$) and matches binary-ours exactly on HellaSwag ($0.6701$)---consistent with higher $\alpha$ progressively approximating binary thresholding.
The fact that even the best graduated configuration does not reach binary-ours confirms that ordinal depth assignments for $d \geq 2$ introduce noise rather than signal (detailed $\alpha$ sweep in Appendix).

\paragraph{Summary.}
$\Kempirical = 2$ correctly predicts that graduated refinement provides no additional benefit.
Binary $\Kstar$-aware filtering is the most effective strategy among all tested approaches: neither ordinal depth weighting nor alternative quality signals (surprisal scoring) improve upon binary curation at the $\Kempirical$ boundary.

