4v5 & 0.5340 & 0.5136 & 0.5045 & 0.5141 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig2_auc_decay.pdf}
\caption{Pairwise $\auc$ decay across depth pairs for model-mode configurations (solid) and decoding strategies (dashed). The horizontal dashed line marks $\theta = 0.60$.}
\label{fig:auc_decay}
\end{figure}

Table~\ref{tab:kstar_ci} and Figure~\ref{fig:auc_decay} show a consistent pattern: 0v1 discrimination is near-perfect ($\auc > 0.99$), followed by a sharp ${\sim}$30\,pp drop at 1v2 ($\auc$: 0.6112--0.6955), with 2v3 falling below $\theta$ (0.5496--0.5893) and deeper pairs converging toward chance.
All four 1v2 CI lower bounds exceed $\theta$ (most marginal: Qwen-continuation at 0.6015), and all four 2v3 CI upper bounds fall below $\theta$, with permutation $p = 0.002$ for all boundary comparisons.
This establishes $\Kempirical = 2$ as a consistent boundary across all tested configurations.

The 1v2 $\auc$ varies across models: Qwen-rewrite (0.6955) $>$ Pythia (0.6539) $>$ OLMo (0.6145) $\approx$ Qwen-continuation (0.6112).
The spread due to generation mode within a single model ($\Delta = 8.4$\,pp) exceeds the spread across models in the same mode ($\Delta = 3.9$\,pp), suggesting generation protocol is a stronger determinant of detectability than model identity.

At $\theta = 0.55$, $\Kempirical = 3$ uniformly; at $\theta = 0.65$, continuation modes drop to $\Kempirical = 1$ while rewrite retains $\Kempirical = 2$; at $\theta = 0.70$, $\Kempirical = 1$ universally.
All four decoding configurations (greedy, nucleus, $T{=}0.70$, $T{=}1.2$) likewise yield $\Kempirical = 2$ at $\theta = 0.60$, with 1v2 $\auc$ in $[0.6866, 0.7039]$ (Table~\ref{tab:s2_decoding}).

%% ====================================================================
\subsection{Cross-Model Transfer}
\label{sec:transfer}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.85\columnwidth]{figures/fig3_transfer_heatmap.pdf}
\caption{Cross-model transfer heatmap (6-class ordinal accuracy). Pythia$\leftrightarrow$OLMo achieves 90--92\% retention; Qwen-rewrite$\to$continuation-mode models: 61--63\%.}
\label{fig:transfer_heatmap}
\end{figure}

Figure~\ref{fig:transfer_heatmap} shows the transfer accuracy matrix (full matrix in Table~\ref{tab:s1_transfer}).
Continuation-mode transfer is high: Pythia$\leftrightarrow$OLMo retains 90--92\% of within-model accuracy, Qwen-cont$\leftrightarrow$Pythia 84--85\%, and Qwen-cont$\leftrightarrow$OLMo 80--85\%.
At the 0v1 boundary, cross-model $\auc$ exceeds 0.96 for all pairs.
These retention rates suggest a large text-intrinsic component of the depth signal.

Qwen-rewrite classifiers transfer poorly (61--63\% retention to Pythia/OLMo), confirming reliance on mode-specific artifacts.
Cross-mode transfer is asymmetric: continuation$\to$rewrite retains 89\% while rewrite$\to$continuation retains 82\%, indicating that continuation-mode features form a generalizable subset.

%% ====================================================================
\subsection{Feature Degradation Ordering}
\label{sec:feature_degradation}

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Top-3 features by single-feature $\auc$ ranking. Bold: shared across all continuation-mode models. Spearman $\rho$ (continuation pairs): Pythia--OLMo 0.95, Pythia--Qwen-cont 0.91, OLMo--Qwen-cont 0.85.}
\label{tab:top_features}
\begin{tabular}{cllll}
\toprule
Rank & Pythia & OLMo & Qwen-rw & Qwen-cont \\
\midrule
1 & \textbf{\texttt{surp\_d1\_std}} & \textbf{\texttt{surp\_std}} & \texttt{freq\_entropy} & \textbf{\texttt{surp\_d1\_std}} \\
2 & \textbf{\texttt{surp\_d2\_std}} & \textbf{\texttt{surp\_d1\_std}} & \textbf{\texttt{surp\_std}} & \textbf{\texttt{surp\_std}} \\
3 & \textbf{\texttt{surp\_std}} & \textbf{\texttt{surp\_d2\_std}} & \textbf{\texttt{surp\_d1\_std}} & \textbf{\texttt{surp\_d2\_std}} \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Among continuation-mode models, single-feature $\auc$ rankings are highly correlated ($\rho \geq 0.85$; Table~\ref{tab:top_features}).
The top-3 features---\texttt{surp\_d1\_std}, \texttt{surp\_std}, \texttt{surp\_d2\_std}---are shared across all three families, all measuring surprisal variability.
This shared ordering suggests a common mechanism: each generation step smooths the token-level surprisal landscape.
Qwen-rewrite diverges, with \texttt{freq\_entropy} as its top feature---corroborating the cross-model transfer findings.
A limitation is that depth-pair-level agreement weakens ($\tau \approx 0.37$ at 1v2), indicating per-feature contributions at the boundary vary across models.

%% ====================================================================
\subsection{Generation Mode and Neural Probe}
\label{sec:generation_mode}

Within Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct, rewrite mode yields 1v2 $\auc = 0.6955$ versus continuation's $0.6112$ ($\Delta = +8.4$\,pp).
The amplified detectability reflects instruction-following artifacts that are discriminable but mode-specific rather than text-intrinsic, as confirmed by the poor cross-model transfer of rewrite-mode classifiers (61--63\% vs.\ 80--92\% for continuation).
Continuation mode is therefore recommended as the canonical condition for cross-model comparisons.

\paragraph{Neural probe.}
Replacing hand-crafted features with frozen Qwen2.5-1.5B hidden states (1536-dim, MLP probe; Table~\ref{tab:s7_neural_probe}), the 2v3 $\auc$ reaches 0.6195 (vs.\ 0.5609 with 15D features), yielding $\Kempirical^{\text{neural}} = 3$.
This confirms that $\Kempirical = 2$ is a representation-dependent lower bound: the 15D feature set does not saturate the available depth signal.
However, the result is preliminary (single dataset, marginal: $+$1.95\,pp above $\theta$) and awaits cross-model validation on Pythia and OLMo (Table~\ref{tab:neural_probe_crossmodel}).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Neural probe pairwise $\auc$ (frozen-encoder MLP). Pythia/OLMo results pending.}
\label{tab:neural_probe_crossmodel}
\begin{tabular}{lccccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Model} & \textbf{0v1} & \textbf{1v2} & \textbf{2v3} & \textbf{3v4} & $\Kempirical^{\text{neural}}$ \\
\midrule
Qwen-1.5B & 0.987 & 0.698 & 0.620 & 0.593 & 3 \\
Pythia-1.4B & \textit{--} & \textit{--} & \textit{--} & \textit{--} & \textit{--} \\
OLMo-1B & \textit{--} & \textit{--} & \textit{--} & \textit{--} & \textit{--} \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\section{Application: $\Kstar$-Aware Data Filtering}
\label{sec:filtering}

$\Kempirical = 2$ correctly predicts that graduated refinement beyond the binary boundary provides no additional benefit over binary filtering.
We compare six data curation strategies on a corpus of 30{,}000 documents (5{,}000 per depth, depths 0--5): (i)~\textbf{no-filter} (uniform weight), (ii)~\textbf{binary-ours} ($w = 1 - P(\text{synthetic})$ from the 15D LightGBM classifier), (iii)~\textbf{binary-Drayson} (importance weighting following \citet{drayson2025}, same classifier, weights $w_j \propto (1 - q(x_j))^b$, $b{=}1$, cap $r_{\max}{=}3$), (iv)~\textbf{graduated} (exponential decay $w = \exp(-\alpha \cdot \hat{d})$, $\alpha \in \{0.1,\ldots,0.9\}$), (v)~\textbf{surprisal} (retain documents above corpus median surprisal), and (vi)~\textbf{DSIR}~\citep{xie2023dsir} (importance resampling against depth-0 reference).

We fine-tune Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct via LoRA (rank~8, $\alpha_{\text{LoRA}}{=}16$) for 3 epochs, evaluating on MMLU (14{,}042 examples, 0-shot) and HellaSwag (10{,}042 examples, 0-shot) across 5 seeds; the graduated strategy is further crossed with 5~$\alpha$ values (51 total runs).

\paragraph{Results.}

\input{tables/tab_filtering_main}

All strategies degrade absolute performance relative to the base model (MMLU 0.6018, HellaSwag 0.6821).
Binary-ours achieves the highest accuracy on both benchmarks (MMLU $0.5941 \pm 0.0009$; HellaSwag $0.6701 \pm 0.0006$), outperforming no-filter by $+$0.76\,pp / $+$0.63\,pp and binary-Drayson ($0.5913$; $0.6691$) by $+$0.28\,pp / $+$0.10\,pp.
Graduated filtering at its best $\alpha$ does not outperform binary-ours on either benchmark.
Surprisal-based selection (MMLU $0.5894 \pm 0.0010$; HellaSwag $0.6641 \pm 0.0006$) falls short by $-$0.47\,pp / $-$0.60\,pp.
DSIR results (\textbf{XX.XX}) are pending.

\paragraph{$\alpha$ sensitivity.}
Higher $\alpha$ values yield monotonically better performance on both benchmarks (HellaSwag: $0.6640$ at $\alpha{=}0.1$ to $0.6701$ at $\alpha{=}0.9$).
At $\alpha{=}0.9$, graduated filtering narrows the MMLU gap to 0.49\,pp below binary-ours ($0.5892$ vs.\ $0.5941$) and matches binary-ours exactly on HellaSwag ($0.6701$)---consistent with higher $\alpha$ progressively approximating binary thresholding.
The fact that even the best graduated configuration does not reach binary-ours confirms that ordinal depth assignments for $d \geq 2$ introduce noise rather than signal (detailed $\alpha$ sweep in Appendix).

\paragraph{Summary.}
$\Kempirical = 2$ correctly predicts that graduated refinement provides no additional benefit.
Binary $\Kstar$-aware filtering is the most effective strategy among all tested approaches: neither ordinal depth weighting nor alternative quality signals (surprisal scoring) improve upon binary curation at the $\Kempirical$ boundary.

\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:discussion}

\paragraph{Interpretation.}
The consistency of $\Kempirical = 2$ across all 8 tested configurations indicates that the two-step information bottleneck is a structural feature of autoregressive generation at the $\leq$1.5B scale.
The feature degradation analysis provides mechanistic evidence: surprisal-variance features degrade in a shared order across model families (Spearman $\rho \geq 0.85$), suggesting that each generation step smooths the token-level surprisal landscape in a model-independent fashion.
The neural probe result ($\Kempirical^{\text{neural}} = 3$ on one dataset) confirms that the 15D boundary is representation-dependent, not fundamental---$\Ktheory \geq 3$.
Extended theoretical discussion, including connections to model collapse theory and scale considerations, appears in Appendix~\ref{sec:extended_discussion}.

\paragraph{Practical implications.}
The implied three-tier taxonomy (human / generation-1 / generation-2+) requires no per-model or per-decoding-strategy calibration.
Cross-model transfer (80--92\% accuracy retention; Section~\ref{sec:transfer}) enables deployment without generator-specific retraining.
Our filtering experiments (Section~\ref{sec:filtering}) show that binary filtering at the $\Kempirical{=}2$ boundary is the most effective strategy among those tested; graduated strategies offer no additional benefit.
Frontier LLMs achieve ${\sim}88\%$ binary detection but only ${\sim}33\%$ five-class depth accuracy (below OBD's 38.6\%), confirming that depth estimation requires specialized features (Appendix~\ref{sec:extended_llm}).

\paragraph{Limitations.}
Our findings are bounded by (1)~model scale ($\leq$1.5B), (2)~prompt-chain rather than retrain-chain protocols (our $\Kempirical$ is a conservative upper bound on retrain-chain $\Kstar$ under monotonic-degradation assumptions), (3)~English C4 text only, (4)~four decoding strategies, (5)~threshold sensitivity ($\Kempirical$ shifts from 3 at $\theta{=}0.55$ to 1 at $\theta{=}0.70$; our conclusions are conditioned on $\theta{=}0.60$), (6)~marginal boundary (Qwen-continuation 1v2 CI lower bound exceeds $\theta$ by only 0.15\,pp), (7)~the 15D feature set yields an operational lower bound ($\Ktheory$ may be strictly higher), and (8)~the neural probe result ($\Kempirical^{\text{neural}}{=}3$) covers a single dataset and awaits cross-model validation.
Detailed limitation analysis appears in Appendix~\ref{sec:extended_limitations}.

